  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "What is the quotient of $2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}-1$ divided by $2{x}^{2}+1?$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the dividend is missing the ${x}^{3}$ and $x$ terms. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing terms with zero coefficients, as follows:\n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}-1=2{x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+0x-1\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}-1 \\\\  & 2{x}^{2}+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+0x-1\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}_{\u2015} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,2{x}^{2}+0x-1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+0x-1}_{\u2015} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is ${x}^{2}-1,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $0.$",
